.TH TRUEFLASHER 1 "2026-11-14" "trueflasher 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trueflasher \- find the real capacity of fake USB flash drives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo trueflasher
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B sudo trueflasher --device
.I /dev/sdX
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
True Flasher measures how much data a USB flash drive or SD card really keeps.
It writes self-identifying 4 KiB pages, re-reads them, and counts what survived.
Run time is proportional to the real size, not the claimed size.
.PP
The test erases the selected drive. Only USB and SD/MMC drives are listed.
The disk holding /, /boot, /home, /usr, /var or active swap is always protected.
The drive is unmounted for you and opened with O_EXCL | O_DIRECT | O_SYNC.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --device " " /dev/sdX
Test this drive (skips the picker; still asks to confirm).
.TP
.BR --quick
Stage 1 only (about 1 minute). Recycling fakes can pass this; use the full test.
.TP
.BR --no-probe
Skip stage 1.
.TP
.BR --check-mb " " N
Spot-check interval during the fill, in MB (default 1024).
.TP
.BR --list
List drives and exit.
.TP
.BR --fix-mb " " N
Do not test: partition the drive to N MB (its real size from an earlier test).
.TP
.BR --label " " TEXT
Volume label for the fix (default "USB <size>GB").
.TP
.BR --any-bus
Also allow non-USB drives and loop devices (system disk stays protected).
.TP
.BR --no-color
Plain output (also honours NO_COLOR).
.SH FIX
After a FAKE verdict, True Flasher can partition the drive to its real size
with sfdisk, format it (mkfs.vfat up to 32 GB, mkfs.exfat above) and label it,
e.g. USB 28GB. Needs sfdisk (util-linux/fdisk) plus dosfstools or exfatprogs.
If one is missing, True Flasher prints the exact commands instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Genuine, or fix completed with no problem found.
.TP
.B 3
Fake drive detected.
.TP
.B 1
Error (I/O failure, drive disappeared, fix tool missing).
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Interactive picker (needs root for raw disk access):
.B sudo trueflasher
.TP
Test a known device:
.B sudo trueflasher --device /dev/sdb
.TP
Quick one minute probe only:
.B sudo trueflasher --device /dev/sdb --quick
.TP
Repair a known fake to its measured size without re-testing:
.B sudo trueflasher --device /dev/sdb --fix-mb 28733
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sfdisk (8),
.BR mkfs.vfat (8),
.BR mkfs.exfat (8)
